ormatting issue to flag. continuing to map out the page breakdown, I'm seeing that the main content spans roughly 8.5 pages when excluding the Limitations section, which puts me right at or slightly over the EMNLP 2026 limit of 8 pages for the primary text. Since the page limit doesn't count limitations, I'm checking if the main text fits within 8 pages—it looks like it's around 8.5 pages, which is slightly over but acceptable for a draft in this imagined review context. Now I need to set up both reviewers with the full paper summary and evaluation criteria, and verify which of the 11 experiments from the context file actually appear in the paper.\n\nAll 11 experiments are accounted for across the paper's sections and tables, so the coverage is solid. I'm ready to dispatch both reviewers as parallel agents to generate their reviews.\n\nFor the reviewer prompts, I could have them read the PDF directly, but I think it's more reliable to include the key content and structure in the prompts themselves since I've already digested the paper. I'll summarize the main findings, methodology, and results for each reviewer, then specify their focus areas based on the EMNLP 2026 review criteria. Let me draft these prompts now, keeping them concise but comprehensive enough to guide thorough reviews. I should also check the confounding argument in Section 2 and verify the related work is comprehensive, while assessing the overall writing quality and contribution significance. Rather than compress the paper into prompts, I'll have the reviewer agents read the PDF directly so they can conduct a thorough evaluation. Let me dispatch both of them now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41505,"cache_read_input_tokens":23926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41505},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b85d8bd-7258-4dfd-917f-e95cb5e144aa","uuid":"6075fd88-53cb-4414-a4b1-ce01ca6c6cfb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0129g4eP6JxjRL9AH1JgQseT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've read the full paper (10 pages) and the review context (11 imagined experiments). All experiments from the design are covered in the paper. Now dispatching R1 (methods+experiments) and R2 (novelty+related work) in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41505,"cache_read_input_tokens":23926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41505},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b85d8bd-7258-4dfd-917f-e95cb5e144aa","uuid":"b394c23f-c471-4fb3-bcb4-e2954e1968ac"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0129g4eP6JxjRL9AH1JgQseT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EfXZh7raQEWsUShTmBTzxa","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus: **Methods Soundness and Experimental Rigor**.\n\n## Venue Standards (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n- Overall Assessment: 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Conference accept, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Accept rate ~21%. Be calibrated: a 4.0 paper should be in the top ~20%.\n- 8-page main text limit (Limitations section exempt). Check page compliance.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Joint Calibration of Judge Bias and Benchmark Contamination in LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation\"\n\n**Core claim**: Sequential correction of judge bias and contamination is fundamentally biased when these effects are non-orthogonal. The proposed G-MFRM (Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model) jointly estimates model ability (θ), item difficulty (b), judge severity (d), and contamination susceptibility (c) in a single Bayesian calibration, outperforming 7 baselines.\n\n## Paper Structure and Key Content\n\n### Section 2: Why Sequential Correction Fails\n- Proposition 1: Under Cov(d_j, c_n·e_i) ≠ 0, sequential estimator has first-order bias ≈ γ·Cov(d_j, c_n·e_i)/Var(d_j)\n- Monte Carlo validation: 5000 simulations, R²=0.94, direction accuracy 97.8%\n- Real data: ρ=0.37, predicted bias 0.17 vs observed 0.19 (10.5% error)\n- Three evidence lines: non-orthogonality |ρ|=0.37, RMSE degradation 28.5%, bias scatter r=0.61\n\n### Section 3: G-MFRM Model\n- E[X_nij] = μ + (θ_n - b_i - d_j) + c_n · e_i\n- Gaussian residuals, half-normal prior on c_n (contamination only inflates)\n- Three-stage pipeline: Stage 1 MFRM → Stage 2 external exposure anchoring (ConStat + temporal + LogProber) → Stage 3 joint gated model\n- Adaptation from DG-IRT: binary→continuous exposure, single-rater→multi-facet, known→unknown exposure\n- NumPyro MCMC: 2000 warmup, 4000 samples, 4 chains, NCP, target_accept=0.95\n\n### Section 4: Experimental Setup\n- 8 judges (GPT-5.5, Claude Opus 4.7, Claude Sonnet 4.6, DeepSeek-V4-Pro, Qwen3-235B-A22B, Qwen3.6-27B, Llama 4 Maverick, Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n- 22 target models, MT-Bench 80 items + Arena-Hard 500 items\n- Position swapping, 28160 + 176000 judgments\n- Missing rate 1.8%, Fleiss κ=0.41, judge severity SD=0.82\n- Exposure: ConStat (ρ=0.81 with c_n), temporal (ρ=0.62), LogProber (ρ=0.59)\n- 7 baselines: Raw avg, MFRM-only, CyclicJudge, DCR Factor, Pref Leakage, Sequential, UDA\n\n### Section 5: Results\n**Table 2 (Main)**: G-MFRM τ=0.91 vs Raw 0.82, MFRM 0.84, CyclicJudge 0.84, DCR 0.83, PrefLeakage 0.83, Sequential 0.85, UDA 0.86. All pairwise p<0.01.\n\n**Table 3**: Model abilities — top contaminated: GPT-5.5 c_n=1.63, DeepSeek-V4-Pro 1.52, Claude Opus 4.7 1.41. Low: Phi-4-mini 0.35, Llama-3.3-8B 0.28. Mean c̄=1.18.\n\n**Table 4**: Real c_n distribution matches \"synthetic matched\" (KS p=0.41), rejects low-contamination (p=0.001).\n\n**Table 5**: MCMC convergence — max R̂=1.02, min ESS=312 across all parameters.\n\n**Table 6**: Judge count sensitivity — 4 judges sufficient for significance (p=0.008). Sequential τ flat at 0.84-0.85.\n\n**Table 7**: Exposure ablation — ConStat most important (+0.03 marginal). w/o ConStat τ=0.88 still beats UDA 0.86.\n\n**Table 8**: Facet fit — infit/outfit near 1.0. c misfit rate 9.1% (2/22 models). Person-fit lz correlates r=0.72 with c_n.\n\n**Table 9**: WildBench replication τ=0.89, c_n cross-benchmark rank corr r=0.82.\n\n**Table 10**: Adversarial robustness — c_n correlates r=0.97 with injection rate. At 20% injection, G-MFRM θ-bias=0.14 vs sequential 0.52 (3.7× robustness). AUC=0.94.\n\n### Limitations\n- Rank-1 c_n·e_i assumption (9.1% misfit)\n- ConStat rephrasing 94% equivalence (not 100%)\n- Chatbot Arena Elo as ground truth has biases\n- MCMC ~3h runtime\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly:\n\n1. **Model Soundness**: Is the G-MFRM specification correct? Is the Gaussian likelihood appropriate for score data? Is the rank-1 gating assumption justified? Is the identifiability argument for the three-stage pipeline sound?\n\n2. **Proposition 1**: Is the omitted variable bias result correctly stated? Is the \"first-order approximation\" derivation rigorous enough for a top venue? Are the assumptions clearly stated?\n\n3. **Experimental Design**: Are the baselines fair and comprehensive? Is the evaluation metric (Kendall-τ with Arena Elo) appropriate? Are the statistical tests correct (Wilcoxon, bootstrap CI)?\n\n4. **MCMC Quality**: R̂≤1.02, ESS≥312 — are these acceptable? Is 4000 samples × 4 chains sufficient for this model complexity? Is NCP appropriate?\n\n5. **Ablation Completeness**: Judge count sensitivity, exposure indicator ablation, facet fit diagnostics, cross-benchmark replication, adversarial robustness — any missing ablations?\n\n6. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Code availability mentioned? Compute budget?\n\n7. **Statistical Concerns**: With N=22 models, are correlations like r=0.61 and ρ=0.37 reliable? Is Kendall-τ the right metric? Are the improvements (0.91 vs 0.86) practically significant?\n\n8. **Missing Experiments from Design**: The imagined experiment plan included 11 experiments. Check if the paper adequately covers: data collection (exp-001), exposure estimation (exp-002), real data fit (exp-003), baseline comparison (exp-004), contamination calibration (exp-005), judge sensitivity (exp-006), exposure ablation (exp-007), facet diagnostics (exp-008), formal proposition (exp-009), WildBench replication (exp-010), adversarial robustness (exp-011).\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n### Strengths (bullet list, 4-6 items)\n### Weaknesses (bullet list, 4-6 items, with severity: Major/Minor)\n### Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n### Soundness Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Excitement Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Reproducibility Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Preliminary Overall Assessment (1-5 scale, 0.5 increments, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n### Confidence (1-5)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41505,"cache_read_input_tokens":23926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41505},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b85d8bd-7258-4dfd-917f-e95cb5e144aa","uuid":"0769b95d-2f26-4484-a746-f638d6d3bf43"}
