{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013n8921ipxAmsatfEwrVWyD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Synthetic data generation for Gated Multi-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM).\n2\t\n3\tGenerates binary response tensors Y[n,i,j] from the model:\n4\t    logit(P_nij) = theta_n - b_i - d_j + c_n * e_i * (1 + s_j)\n5\t\n6\tTwo confounding mechanisms controlled by rho:\n7\t  A) Item-level: e_i correlated with -b_i (easy items get high exposure).\n8\t  B) Judge-level: s_j (contamination sensitivity) correlated with -d_j\n9\t     (strict judges dampen contamination signal), so d̂_j absorbs part of\n10\t     the c_n·e_i effect when contamination is unmodeled.\n11\t\n12\tDependencies: numpy, scipy\n13\t\"\"\"\n14\t\n15\timport numpy as np\n16\tfrom scipy.special import expit\n17\t\n18\t\n19\tdef generate_gated_mfrm_data(\n20\t    N: int = 20,\n21\t    I: int = 100,\n22\t    J: int = 5,\n23\t    rho: float = 0.3,\n24\t    seed: int = 42,\n25\t) -> dict:\n26\t    \"\"\"Generate synthetic G-MFRM data with item-level and judge-level confounding.\n27\t\n28\t    Args:\n29\t        N: Number of models (examinees).\n30\t        I: Number of items.\n31\t        J: Number of judges.\n32\t        rho: Controls both confounding strengths: (A) corr(e_i, -b_i) for\n33\t             item-level and (B) corr(s_j, -d_j) for judge-level.\n34\t        seed: Random seed for reproducibility.\n35\t\n36\t    Returns:\n37\t        Dict with keys:\n38\t            responses: (N, I, J) binary numpy array.\n39\t            true_params: dict of theta (N,), b (I,), d (J,), c (N,), e (I,), s (J,).\n40\t            config: dict of N, I, J, rho, seed.\n41\t    \"\"\"\n42\t    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n43\t\n44\t    theta = rng.standard_normal(N)\n45\t    b = rng.standard_normal(I)\n46\t    d = rng.normal(0, 0.5, size=J)\n47\t    c = np.abs(rng.normal(0, 0.5, size=N))\n48\t\n49\t    # Confounding A (item-level): e_i correlated with -b_i (easy items get high exposure)\n50\t    b_std = b.std()\n51\t    if b_std < 1e-8:\n52\t        b_std = 1.0\n53\t    z = rng.standard_normal(I)\n54\t    e_raw = rho * (-b / b_std) + np.sqrt(1 - rho**2) * z\n55\t    e = expit(e_raw)  # map to [0, 1]\n56\t\n57\t    # Confounding B (judge-level): s_j correlated with -d_j\n58\t    # Strict judges (high d_j) get negative s_j → dampen contamination signal\n59\t    d_std = d.std()\n60\t    if d_std < 1e-8:\n61\t        d_std = 1.0\n62\t    z_s = rng.standard_normal(J)\n63\t    s = 0.3 * (-rho * (d / d_std) + np.sqrt(1 - rho**2) * z_s)\n64\t\n65\t    # logit(P_nij) = theta_n - b_i - d_j + c_n * e_i * (1 + s_j)\n66\t    contamination = c[:, None, None] * e[None, :, None] * (1.0 + s[None, None, :])\n67\t    logits = (\n68\t        theta[:, None, None]\n69\t        - b[None, :, None]\n70\t        - d[None, None, :]\n71\t        + contamination\n72\t    )\n73\t    probs = expit(logits)\n74\t    responses = rng.binomial(1, probs).astype(np.int8)\n75\t\n76\t    return {\n77\t        \"responses\": responses,\n78\t        \"true_params\": {\n79\t            \"theta\": theta,\n80\t            \"b\": b,\n81\t            \"d\": d,\n82\t            \"c\": c,\n83\t            \"e\": e,\n84\t            \"s\": s,\n85\t        },\n86\t        \"config\": {\"N\": N, \"I\": I, \"J\": J, \"rho\": rho, \"seed\": seed},\n87\t    }\n88\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EfXZh7raQEWsUShTmBTzxa","session_id":"7b85d8bd-7258-4dfd-917f-e95cb5e144aa","uuid":"481c1e17-c5bf-4dcb-96c6-97145982b7a2","timestamp":"2026-05-21T21:18:19.868Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0129g4eP6JxjRL9AH1JgQseT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A1TLRBFFn17v4XNYciSLcZ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus: **Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue Standards (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n- Overall Assessment: 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Conference accept, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Accept rate ~21%. Be calibrated: a 4.0 paper should be in the top ~20%.\n- 8-page main text limit (Limitations section exempt).\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Joint Calibration of Judge Bias and Benchmark Contamination in LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation\"\n\n**Core contribution**: G-MFRM = MFRM (Many-Facet Rasch Model) + DG-IRT gating mechanism, adapted to LLM evaluation with continuous exposure, multi-facet calibration, and external anchoring. Shows sequential correction is biased (Proposition 1) and joint calibration fixes it.\n\n## Full Paper Content\n\n### Abstract\nLLM-as-a-judge evaluation suffers from inter-judge severity bias and benchmark contamination. Existing methods address these independently or sequentially. Paper shows sequential correction is fundamentally flawed when d_j and c_n·e_i are non-orthogonal. Proposes G-MFRM for joint Bayesian calibration. Results: τ=0.91 vs best baseline UDA τ=0.86 (p<0.001) on MT-Bench + Arena-Hard with 8 judges, 22 target models. Also works on WildBench (τ=0.89). AUC=0.94 for contamination detection.\n\n### Section 1: Introduction\n- LLM-as-a-judge has two distortions: judge severity bias and benchmark contamination\n- Research community addresses them in separate silos\n- CyclicJudge, UDA = bias correction; DCR, Preference Leakage = contamination\n- Sequential correction fails due to omitted variable confounding\n- Real data: |ρ|=0.37, RMSE degradation 28.5%, bias scatter r=0.61\n\n**Three contributions**:\n1. Formal analysis showing sequential correction is asymptotically biased (Proposition 1, MC validated R²=0.94)\n2. G-MFRM: first framework jointly estimating θ, b, d, c\n3. Comprehensive evaluation: τ=0.91 vs 0.86 best baseline, ablations, sensitivity, adversarial tests\n\n### Section 2: Why Sequential Correction Fails\n- Proposition 1: bias-first sequential estimator has E[θ̂_seq - θ] ≈ γ·Cov(d_j, c_n·e_i)/Var(d_j)\n- \"follows from standard omitted variable bias analysis adapted to the psychometric setting\"\n- MC validation: 5000 sims, R²=0.94, 97.8% direction accuracy\n- Real data validation: predicted bias 0.17 vs observed 0.19\n\n### Section 3: The Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model\n- E[X_nij] = μ + (θ_n - b_i - d_j) + c_n · e_i\n- Gaussian residuals\n- Three-stage pipeline: Stage 1 MFRM → Stage 2 external exposure anchoring → Stage 3 joint model\n- DG-IRT adaptation: binary→continuous, single-rater→multi-facet, known→unknown exposure\n\n### Section 4: Experimental Setup\n- 8 LLM judges, 4 providers, API + open-source\n- 22 target models, MT-Bench 80 + Arena-Hard 500 items\n- Exposure estimation: ConStat + temporal + LogProber composite\n- 7 baselines across three categories\n\n### Section 5: Results (Tables 2-10)\n- Main: G-MFRM τ=0.91 vs all baselines (next best UDA 0.86)\n- Mean c̄_n=1.18, real distribution matches synthetic \"matched\" scenario\n- MCMC: R̂≤1.02, ESS≥312\n- 4 judges sufficient for significance\n- ConStat dominant exposure indicator; w/o ConStat still beats all baselines\n- WildBench: τ=0.89, c_n cross-benchmark r=0.82\n- Adversarial: 3.7× more robust than sequential, AUC=0.94\n\n### Section 6: Related Work\nThree paragraphs:\n1. **LLM Judge Bias**: Zheng et al. 2023 (position/verbosity bias), CyclicJudge (Lin et al. 2025), UDA (Li et al. 2024a)\n2. **Benchmark Contamination**: ConStat (De Koninck et al. 2024), DCR (Yang et al. 2024), Preference Leakage (Li et al. 2024b), LogProber\n3. **Psychometric Models**: IRT (Polo et al. 2024), MFRM (Linacre 1989), DG-IRT (Robitzsch 2020)\n\n### References (10 total)\n\n### Limitations\n- Rank-1 c_n·e_i assumption\n- ConStat 94% semantic equivalence\n- Chatbot Arena Elo as ground truth\n- MCMC ~3h runtime\n\n## Domain Context for Calibrating Novelty\n\nKey existing work in this space:\n- **CyclicJudge** (AAAI 2025): Round-robin to eliminate average judge bias\n- **UDA** (ACL 2024): Neural K-factor for debiasing, 63.4% SD reduction, black-box\n- **DCR** (EMNLP 2024): Decontaminated ranking\n- **Preference Leakage** (NeurIPS 2024): LLM judges prefer contaminated outputs\n- **ConStat** (ICML 2024): Behavioral contamination detection via rephrasing\n- **TinyBenchmarks/IRT** (ICML 2024): IRT for LLM evaluation\n- **MFRM** (Linacre 1989): Classic psychometric model for multi-facet calibration\n- **DG-IRT** (Robitzsch 2020): Differential group IRT for DIF detection\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**:\n   - Is combining MFRM + DG-IRT gating novel enough for a top venue?\n   - Are the three adaptations (binary→continuous, single→multi-facet, known→unknown) individually non-trivial?\n   - Is Proposition 1 novel, or is it a straightforward application of omitted variable bias?\n   - Does the paper offer conceptual insight beyond \"joint > sequential\"?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**:\n   - Only 10 references total — is this sufficient?\n   - Missing important related work? Consider: other IRT-based LLM evaluation papers, DIF literature, test contamination in educational testing, Bayesian psychometric models\n   - Is the positioning against baselines fair and accurate?\n\n3. **Framing and Motivation**:\n   - Is the confounding argument compelling?\n   - Is the paper well-motivated for the NLP community?\n   - Does the introduction overclaim or appropriately scope?\n\n4. **Writing Quality**:\n   - Is the paper clearly written?\n   - Is the notation consistent?\n   - Is the paper well-organized?\n   - Any clarity issues?\n\n5. **Significance and Impact**:\n   - Will this change how the community does LLM evaluation?\n   - Is the practical barrier to adoption (8 judges, MCMC 3h) too high?\n   - Is the improvement (τ: 0.86→0.91) meaningful in practice?\n\n6. **Page Limit Compliance**:\n   - Main text is Sections 1-7 (Conclusion). Limitations is exempt.\n   - Does main text fit within 8 pages?\n\n7. **Anonymity and Format**:\n   - Any anonymity violations?\n   - Proper use of anonymous submission conventions?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n### Strengths (bullet list, 4-6 items)\n### Weaknesses (bullet list, 4-6 items, with severity: Major/Minor)\n### Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n### Novelty Assessment (detailed paragraph)\n### Related Work Gaps\n### Soundness Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Excitement Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Reproducibility Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Preliminary Overall Assessment (1-5 scale, 0.5 increments, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n### Confidence (1-5)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41505,"cache_read_input_tokens":23926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41505},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b85d8bd-7258-4dfd-917f-e95cb5e144aa","uuid":"ca9055c7-8e20-418f-913b-7ee9fb377a7f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a7f5ddd906765a49a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01A1TLRBFFn17v4XNYciSLcZ","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus: **Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue Standards (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n- Overall Assessment: 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Conference accept, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Accept rate ~21%. Be calibrated: a 4.0 paper should be in the top ~20%.\n- 8-page main text limit (Limitations section exempt).\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Joint Calibration of Judge Bias and Benchmark Contamination in LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation\"\n\n**Core contribution**: G-MFRM = MFRM (Many-Facet Rasch Model) + DG-IRT gating mechanism, adapted to LLM evaluation with continuous exposure, multi-facet calibration, and external anchoring. Shows sequential correction is biased (Proposition 1) and joint calibration fixes it.\n\n## Full Paper Content\n\n### Abstract\nLLM-as-a-judge evaluation suffers from inter-judge severity bias and benchmark contamination. Existing methods address these independently or sequentially. Paper shows sequential correction is fundamentally flawed when d_j and c_n·e_i are non-orthogonal. Proposes G-MFRM for joint Bayesian calibration. Results: τ=0.91 vs best baseline UDA τ=0.86 (p<0.001) on MT-Bench + Arena-Hard with 8 judges, 22 target models. Also works on WildBench (τ=0.89). AUC=0.94 for contamination detection.\n\n### Section 1: Introduction\n- LLM-as-a-judge has two distortions: judge severity bias and benchmark contamination\n- Research community addresses them in separate silos\n- CyclicJudge, UDA = bias correction; DCR, Preference Leakage = contamination\n- Sequential correction fails due to omitted variable confounding\n- Real data: |ρ|=0.37, RMSE degradation 28.5%, bias scatter r=0.61\n\n**Three contributions**:\n1. Formal analysis showing sequential correction is asymptotically biased (Proposition 1, MC validated R²=0.94)\n2. G-MFRM: first framework jointly estimating θ, b, d, c\n3. Comprehensive evaluation: τ=0.91 vs 0.86 best baseline, ablations, sensitivity, adversarial tests\n\n### Section 2: Why Sequential Correction Fails\n- Proposition 1: bias-first sequential estimator has E[θ̂_seq - θ] ≈ γ·Cov(d_j, c_n·e_i)/Var(d_j)\n- \"follows from standard omitted variable bias analysis adapted to the psychometric setting\"\n- MC validation: 5000 sims, R²=0.94, 97.8% direction accuracy\n- Real data validation: predicted bias 0.17 vs observed 0.19\n\n### Section 3: The Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model\n- E[X_nij] = μ + (θ_n - b_i - d_j) + c_n · e_i\n- Gaussian residuals\n- Three-stage pipeline: Stage 1 MFRM → Stage 2 external exposure anchoring → Stage 3 joint model\n- DG-IRT adaptation: binary→continuous, single-rater→multi-facet, known→unknown exposure\n\n### Section 4: Experimental Setup\n- 8 LLM judges, 4 providers, API + open-source\n- 22 target models, MT-Bench 80 + Arena-Hard 500 items\n- Exposure estimation: ConStat + temporal + LogProber composite\n- 7 baselines across three categories\n\n### Section 5: Results (Tables 2-10)\n- Main: G-MFRM τ=0.91 vs all baselines (next best UDA 0.86)\n- Mean c̄_n=1.18, real distribution matches synthetic \"matched\" scenario\n- MCMC: R̂≤1.02, ESS≥312\n- 4 judges sufficient for significance\n- ConStat dominant exposure indicator; w/o ConStat still beats all baselines\n- WildBench: τ=0.89, c_n cross-benchmark r=0.82\n- Adversarial: 3.7× more robust than sequential, AUC=0.94\n\n### Section 6: Related Work\nThree paragraphs:\n1. **LLM Judge Bias**: Zheng et al. 2023 (position/verbosity bias), CyclicJudge (Lin et al. 2025), UDA (Li et al. 2024a)\n2. **Benchmark Contamination**: ConStat (De Koninck et al. 2024), DCR (Yang et al. 2024), Preference Leakage (Li et al. 2024b), LogProber\n3. **Psychometric Models**: IRT (Polo et al. 2024), MFRM (Linacre 1989), DG-IRT (Robitzsch 2020)\n\n### References (10 total)\n\n### Limitations\n- Rank-1 c_n·e_i assumption\n- ConStat 94% semantic equivalence\n- Chatbot Arena Elo as ground truth\n- MCMC ~3h runtime\n\n## Domain Context for Calibrating Novelty\n\nKey existing work in this space:\n- **CyclicJudge** (AAAI 2025): Round-robin to eliminate average judge bias\n- **UDA** (ACL 2024): Neural K-factor for debiasing, 63.4% SD reduction, black-box\n- **DCR** (EMNLP 2024): Decontaminated ranking\n- **Preference Leakage** (NeurIPS 2024): LLM judges prefer contaminated outputs\n- **ConStat** (ICML 2024): Behavioral contamination detection via rephrasing\n- **TinyBenchmarks/IRT** (ICML 2024): IRT for LLM evaluation\n- **MFRM** (Linacre 1989): Classic psychometric model for multi-facet calibration\n- **DG-IRT** (Robitzsch 2020): Differential group IRT for DIF detection\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**:\n   - Is combining MFRM + DG-IRT gating novel enough for a top venue?\n   - Are the three adaptations (binary→continuous, single→multi-facet, known→unknown) individually non-trivial?\n   - Is Proposition 1 novel, or is it a straightforward application of omitted variable bias?\n   - Does the paper offer conceptual insight beyond \"joint > sequential\"?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**:\n   - Only 10 references total — is this sufficient?\n   - Missing important related work? Consider: other IRT-based LLM evaluation papers, DIF literature, test contamination in educational testing, Bayesian psychometric models\n   - Is the positioning against baselines fair and accurate?\n\n3. **Framing and Motivation**:\n   - Is the confounding argument compelling?\n   - Is the paper well-motivated for the NLP community?\n   - Does the introduction overclaim or appropriately scope?\n\n4. **Writing Quality**:\n   - Is the paper clearly written?\n   - Is the notation consistent?\n   - Is the paper well-organized?\n   - Any clarity issues?\n\n5. **Significance and Impact**:\n   - Will this change how the community does LLM evaluation?\n   - Is the practical barrier to adoption (8 judges, MCMC 3h) too high?\n   - Is the improvement (τ: 0.86→0.91) meaningful in practice?\n\n6. **Page Limit Compliance**:\n   - Main text is Sections 1-7 (Conclusion). Limitations is exempt.\n   - Does main text fit within 8 pages?\n\n7. **Anonymity and Format**:\n   - Any anonymity violations?\n   - Proper use of anonymous submission conventions?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n### Strengths (bullet list, 4-6 items)\n### Weaknesses (bullet list, 4-6 items, with severity: Major/Minor)\n### Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n### Novelty Assessment (detailed paragraph)\n### Related Work Gaps\n### Soundness Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Excitement Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Reproducibility Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Preliminary Overall Assessment (1-5 scale, 0.5 increments, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n### Confidence (1-5)\n","uuid":"5ee57d8d-0dc0-4704-967c-ff494b6d68ed","session_id":"7b85d8bd-7258-4dfd-917f-e95cb5e144aa"}
