{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A1TLRBFFn17v4XNYciSLcZ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have all the information needed to write a thorough review. Here is my assessment:\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 -- Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n**Paper**: \"G-MFRM: Joint Estimation of Judge Bias and Benchmark Contamination for Fair LLM Evaluation\"\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated problem formulation.** The paper identifies a genuine gap: no prior work (0/12 surveyed papers) jointly models judge severity bias and benchmark contamination. The omitted variable confounding framing is clean and immediately communicates why sequential correction is insufficient. The toy example in Section 2.3 is effective pedagogy.\n\n- **Principled psychometric grounding.** Combining MFRM with DG-IRT gating is a reasonable and well-motivated design choice. The three-stage estimation pipeline (MFRM init, exposure anchoring, joint Bayesian estimation) addresses the identifiability challenge of unknown exposure status in a structured way. The paper correctly identifies that educational testing DG-IRT assumes known exposure, and the external anchoring via ConStat is a non-trivial adaptation.\n\n- **Honest reporting of effect sizes.** The paper does not hide that the empirical improvement is modest (delta-tau = 0.02 on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard; non-significant on WildBench). The conclusion explicitly acknowledges this and reframes the contribution around methodological correctness and diagnostic value. This is a strength of scientific integrity.\n\n- **Graceful degradation property.** Demonstrating that G-MFRM reduces to standard MFRM when contamination is absent eliminates an important adoption concern. The WildBench results (12% high-exposure items, non-significant improvement) serve as a natural \"negative control.\"\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental design.** 8 judges from 4 model families, 22 target models, 203K evaluations, position swapping, ablation study, judge count sensitivity, adversarial robustness, cross-benchmark validation -- this is thorough for a methods paper.\n\n- **Interpretable outputs.** Per-model c_n estimates with cross-benchmark consistency (r = 0.68--0.71), per-judge severity profiles, and per-item difficulty -- these have standalone diagnostic value beyond ranking correction.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Proposition 1 is a restatement of classical omitted variable bias, not a novel theoretical contribution.** The bias expression Cov(d_j, c_n * e_i) / Var(d_j) is the textbook OVB formula from econometrics (Angrist & Pischke 2009, Chapter 3; or any introductory econometrics text). The claim that this \"follows from standard omitted variable bias analysis adapted to the psychometric setting\" (Section 2 of the abstract summary) is accurate but undermines the novelty claim. Calling this a \"Proposition\" with Monte Carlo \"validation\" overstates the contribution -- the OVB formula does not need 5000 simulations to validate; it is an algebraic identity under the assumed linear model. The paper should clearly attribute this to the OVB literature and position the contribution as *applying* this known result to identify a previously unrecognized confounding in LLM evaluation, rather than presenting it as a new theoretical result.\n\n- **(Major) Extremely thin related work with only ~10 references.** For a paper that bridges psychometrics, LLM evaluation, and contamination detection, 10 references is far below the expected density for EMNLP. Critical missing citations include: (1) Polo et al. 2024 \"TinyBenchmarks\" (IRT for efficient LLM evaluation, ICML 2024) -- directly relevant as an IRT-based LLM evaluation paper; (2) Sedoc & Ungar 2020 (item response theory for NLP) -- foundational IRT-in-NLP work; (3) Wang et al. 2025 \"Comparing Human and AI Rater Effects Using MFRM\" (arXiv 2505.18486) -- the most direct predecessor applying MFRM to LLM raters, which the literature_map.yaml lists as the \"most direct predecessor\" yet it is absent from the paper; (4) the DIF (differential item functioning) literature which is the psychometric analog of what G-MFRM does; (5) the broader test contamination literature from educational measurement (e.g., Eckerly 2017, test security and item exposure); (6) Fiedler 2026 on bias and uncertainty in LLM-as-a-judge. The paper's own literature map identifies 12 papers, but only ~10 appear in the references. This is a serious gap.\n\n- **(Major) Modest empirical improvement raises questions about practical significance.** Delta-tau = 0.02 on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard, with p = 0.008 and p = 0.041 respectively. The WildBench result is non-significant (p = 0.062). While the paper argues that methodological correctness matters independent of effect size, reviewers will reasonably ask: if real-world contamination is in the \"mild\" regime (mean c_n = 0.62, closer to MVP-001), is the joint estimation apparatus justified for a 0.02 tau improvement? The 3.2-hour MCMC runtime and 4-judge minimum requirement create a substantial practical barrier relative to the gain. The paper would benefit from a clearer demonstration of settings where the improvement is practically meaningful.\n\n- **(Minor) Discrepancy between abstract summary and paper content.** The task description states tau = 0.91, AUC = 0.94, WildBench tau = 0.89; the actual median paper shows tau = 0.86, detection = 0.68 overall, WildBench tau = 0.84. This appears to reflect different versions of the paper (the \"best\" dream scenario). The review is based on the actual paper tex, but the authors should ensure consistency across all presentation materials.\n\n- **(Minor) The \"gating\" terminology is misleading.** In DG-IRT, the gating mechanism involves a binary indicator that activates/deactivates a latent component. In G-MFRM, there is no actual gate -- the contamination term c_n * e_i is simply an additive component that is always active (just small when e_i is near zero). Calling this \"gated\" suggests a mixture model or threshold mechanism that does not exist. The paper should clarify this or use more precise terminology.\n\n- **(Minor) Model formulation switches between ordinal and continuous without clear justification.** Equation 1 uses logistic MFRM (ordinal categories with thresholds tau_k), but the abstract summary mentions \"Gaussian residuals\" and \"binary to continuous\" adaptation. The paper should clearly specify whether the final G-MFRM uses an ordinal or continuous response model, and justify the choice given that MT-Bench uses 1-10 integer scores.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On Proposition 1**: Can you clarify what is novel beyond standard OVB? If the linear model E[X_nij] = mu + theta_n - b_i - d_j + c_n * e_i + epsilon is assumed, the OVB formula is immediate. Is the contribution (a) showing that OVB applies in this psychometric setting, or (b) showing that d_j and c_n * e_i are empirically non-orthogonal? If (b), the |rho| = 0.37 finding is the actual contribution, not the Proposition.\n\n2. **On exposure estimation circularity**: ConStat computes contamination effects by comparing original vs. rephrased item performance. G-MFRM then uses these ConStat deltas as exposure indicators to estimate contamination susceptibility c_n. How do you ensure that errors in ConStat's contamination signal do not propagate through the G-MFRM pipeline? What is the sensitivity of c_n estimates to noise in e_i?\n\n3. **On the 4-judge minimum**: Table 7 shows that with 2 judges, G-MFRM has a 65% convergence rate and zero improvement. Many LLM evaluation setups use 1-3 judges. Can you discuss the identifiability conditions more formally -- what specifically about 4 judges enables separation of d_j from c_n * e_i?\n\n4. **On Wang et al. 2025 (MFRM for LLM raters)**: Your literature map identifies this as the \"most direct predecessor.\" Why is it not cited in the paper? How does G-MFRM relate to and extend their work?\n\n5. **On the choice of ground truth**: Chatbot Arena Elo is used as the gold standard, but Arena rankings themselves may be affected by contamination (models that perform well on benchmarks attract more Arena traffic). How sensitive are the Kendall-tau results to this potential circularity?\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n\nThe novelty is real but narrower than presented. The paper's contribution is best understood as a *useful transfer* rather than a fundamental methodological advance. The key insight -- that judge bias and contamination confound each other -- is correct and valuable for the LLM evaluation community, but the formalization via OVB is standard. The G-MFRM model itself is a relatively direct combination of MFRM (well-established in psychometrics since 1989) and DG-IRT gating (Robitzsch 2020 / Shu et al. 2013). The three adaptations (binary-to-continuous, single-to-multi-facet, known-to-unknown exposure) are individually incremental: the first two are straightforward extensions, and the third (external anchoring) is essentially \"run ConStat first, then use its output.\" The most novel aspect is arguably the *problem identification* -- demonstrating that the two correction communities are producing confounded estimates -- rather than the solution itself. This is sufficient for a solid contribution but not at the level of conceptual novelty that would merit a top-tier venue award.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n\nSignificant gaps for a paper at this intersection:\n\n1. **Wang et al. 2025 (arXiv 2505.18486)** -- MFRM applied to 10 LLM raters vs. human experts. Most direct predecessor; absence is notable.\n2. **Polo et al. 2024 \"TinyBenchmarks\"** (ICML 2024) -- IRT for efficient LLM evaluation; relevant as an IRT-in-LLM-evaluation paper.\n3. **Sedoc & Ungar 2020** -- Item response theory for NLP evaluation; foundational reference.\n4. **DIF literature** (Holland & Wainer 1993; Zumbo 1999) -- Differential item functioning is the psychometric concept that G-MFRM's contamination term operationalizes. This connection should be made explicit.\n5. **Educational test security** literature (Eckerly 2017; Yi et al. 2008) -- Test contamination has been studied for decades in educational measurement; the parallel should be drawn.\n6. **Fiedler 2026** (arXiv 2605.06939) -- Bias and uncertainty diagnostics for LLM-as-a-judge; directly related.\n7. **Soumik 2026** (arXiv 2604.23178) -- Systematic evaluation of bias mitigation strategies; empirical complement.\n8. **Lin 2026 PSN-IRT** (AAAI 2026) -- Neural 4PL IRT for LLM benchmarking; IRT-based baseline.\n9. **Bayesian IRT models** (e.g., Natesan et al. 2016) -- The Bayesian estimation approach used in Stage 3 has precedent.\n10. Total reference count should be 25-35 for a paper of this scope at EMNLP.\n\n### Soundness Score: 3 / 5\n\nThe theoretical contribution (Proposition 1) is correct but not novel -- it is a direct application of OVB. The experimental design is thorough, but the main improvement (delta-tau = 0.02) is marginal and the WildBench result is non-significant. Convergence diagnostics are adequately reported (R-hat < 1.10, ESS > 100, 0.075% divergent transitions), though the 8% of parameters with R-hat > 1.05 and the 18% contamination misfit rate suggest the model does not fully capture the data-generating process. The three-stage pipeline introduces dependence on ConStat that is not fully analyzed for error propagation.\n\n### Excitement Score: 3 / 5\n\nThe problem identification (confounding between judge bias and contamination correction) is genuinely useful for the LLM evaluation community. The framework is principled. However, the empirical evidence suggests that current contamination levels make the joint correction practically unnecessary (WildBench non-significant; mean c_n = 0.62 in the \"mild\" regime). The paper reads more as a monitoring tool for future contamination than a solution to a current pressing problem. The 4-judge minimum and 3-hour runtime limit immediate adoption. This is a solid \"Findings\" paper that identifies an important methodological issue, but the impact is currently bounded by the empirical reality that contamination is not yet severe enough for joint correction to matter much.\n\n### Reproducibility Score: 3 / 5\n\nThe three-stage pipeline is described at a reasonable level, but several implementation details are missing: the specific NumPyro model code, prior distributions for all parameters (only c_n's half-normal prior is mentioned), the composite exposure weighting scheme, and the ConStat rephrasing procedure. The 203K evaluations represent substantial API costs that limit replication. No code or data release is mentioned. The 8-judge panel with position swapping is well-specified.\n\n### Preliminary Overall Assessment: 3.0\n\nCalibration: This is a methodologically sound paper that identifies a real problem (confounding in sequential LLM evaluation correction) and proposes a principled solution. The problem identification and formalization are the primary contributions. However, the novelty is incremental (OVB + MFRM + DG-IRT combination), the empirical gains are modest (delta-tau = 0.02, non-significant on WildBench), the related work is substantially incomplete (10 references for a cross-disciplinary paper), and the practical impact is limited by the current mild contamination regime. This is appropriate for Findings -- a useful contribution to the evaluation methodology literature that does not reach the threshold for the main conference. To reach 3.5-4.0, the paper would need: (a) substantially expanded related work with proper positioning against Wang 2025, DIF literature, and IRT-in-NLP work; (b) either stronger empirical results or a more compelling case for why the methodological contribution alone justifies acceptance; (c) clearer scoping of Proposition 1 as applied OVB rather than a novel result.\n\n### Confidence: 4 / 5\n\nI am familiar with both the LLM evaluation literature and the psychometric models (IRT, MFRM, DIF) referenced in this paper. My main uncertainty is around the practical significance threshold -- whether delta-tau = 0.02 is considered meaningful in the LLM evaluation community, where leaderboard differences of this magnitude may or may not affect downstream decisions."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7f5ddd906765a49a (use SendMessage with to: 'a7f5ddd906765a49a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 48021\ntool_uses: 15\nduration_ms: 151092</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b85d8bd-7258-4dfd-917f-e95cb5e144aa","uuid":"adaa7d39-1b85-4b2e-a23b-91aa92ba2b64","timestamp":"2026-05-21T21:20:54.697Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus: **Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue Standards (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n- Overall Assessment: 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Conference accept, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Accept rate ~21%. Be calibrated: a 4.0 paper should be in the top ~20%.\n- 8-page main text limit (Limitations section exempt).\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Joint Calibration of Judge Bias and Benchmark Contamination in LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation\"\n\n**Core contribution**: G-MFRM = MFRM (Many-Facet Rasch Model) + DG-IRT gating mechanism, adapted to LLM evaluation with continuous exposure, multi-facet calibration, and external anchoring. Shows sequential correction is biased (Proposition 1) and joint calibration fixes it.\n\n## Full Paper Content\n\n### Abstract\nLLM-as-a-judge evaluation suffers from inter-judge severity bias and benchmark contamination. Existing methods address these independently or sequentially. Paper shows sequential correction is fundamentally flawed when d_j and c_n·e_i are non-orthogonal. Proposes G-MFRM for joint Bayesian calibration. Results: τ=0.91 vs best baseline UDA τ=0.86 (p<0.001) on MT-Bench + Arena-Hard with 8 judges, 22 target models. Also works on WildBench (τ=0.89). AUC=0.94 for contamination detection.\n\n### Section 1: Introduction\n- LLM-as-a-judge has two distortions: judge severity bias and benchmark contamination\n- Research community addresses them in separate silos\n- CyclicJudge, UDA = bias correction; DCR, Preference Leakage = contamination\n- Sequential correction fails due to omitted variable confounding\n- Real data: |ρ|=0.37, RMSE degradation 28.5%, bias scatter r=0.61\n\n**Three contributions**:\n1. Formal analysis showing sequential correction is asymptotically biased (Proposition 1, MC validated R²=0.94)\n2. G-MFRM: first framework jointly estimating θ, b, d, c\n3. Comprehensive evaluation: τ=0.91 vs 0.86 best baseline, ablations, sensitivity, adversarial tests\n\n### Section 2: Why Sequential Correction Fails\n- Proposition 1: bias-first sequential estimator has E[θ̂_seq - θ] ≈ γ·Cov(d_j, c_n·e_i)/Var(d_j)\n- \"follows from standard omitted variable bias analysis adapted to the psychometric setting\"\n- MC validation: 5000 sims, R²=0.94, 97.8% direction accuracy\n- Real data validation: predicted bias 0.17 vs observed 0.19\n\n### Section 3: The Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model\n- E[X_nij] = μ + (θ_n - b_i - d_j) + c_n · e_i\n- Gaussian residuals\n- Three-stage pipeline: Stage 1 MFRM → Stage 2 external exposure anchoring → Stage 3 joint model\n- DG-IRT adaptation: binary→continuous, single-rater→multi-facet, known→unknown exposure\n\n### Section 4: Experimental Setup\n- 8 LLM judges, 4 providers, API + open-source\n- 22 target models, MT-Bench 80 + Arena-Hard 500 items\n- Exposure estimation: ConStat + temporal + LogProber composite\n- 7 baselines across three categories\n\n### Section 5: Results (Tables 2-10)\n- Main: G-MFRM τ=0.91 vs all baselines (next best UDA 0.86)\n- Mean c̄_n=1.18, real distribution matches synthetic \"matched\" scenario\n- MCMC: R̂≤1.02, ESS≥312\n- 4 judges sufficient for significance\n- ConStat dominant exposure indicator; w/o ConStat still beats all baselines\n- WildBench: τ=0.89, c_n cross-benchmark r=0.82\n- Adversarial: 3.7× more robust than sequential, AUC=0.94\n\n### Section 6: Related Work\nThree paragraphs:\n1. **LLM Judge Bias**: Zheng et al. 2023 (position/verbosity bias), CyclicJudge (Lin et al. 2025), UDA (Li et al. 2024a)\n2. **Benchmark Contamination**: ConStat (De Koninck et al. 2024), DCR (Yang et al. 2024), Preference Leakage (Li et al. 2024b), LogProber\n3. **Psychometric Models**: IRT (Polo et al. 2024), MFRM (Linacre 1989), DG-IRT (Robitzsch 2020)\n\n### References (10 total)\n\n### Limitations\n- Rank-1 c_n·e_i assumption\n- ConStat 94% semantic equivalence\n- Chatbot Arena Elo as ground truth\n- MCMC ~3h runtime\n\n## Domain Context for Calibrating Novelty\n\nKey existing work in this space:\n- **CyclicJudge** (AAAI 2025): Round-robin to eliminate average judge bias\n- **UDA** (ACL 2024): Neural K-factor for debiasing, 63.4% SD reduction, black-box\n- **DCR** (EMNLP 2024): Decontaminated ranking\n- **Preference Leakage** (NeurIPS 2024): LLM judges prefer contaminated outputs\n- **ConStat** (ICML 2024): Behavioral contamination detection via rephrasing\n- **TinyBenchmarks/IRT** (ICML 2024): IRT for LLM evaluation\n- **MFRM** (Linacre 1989): Classic psychometric model for multi-facet calibration\n- **DG-IRT** (Robitzsch 2020): Differential group IRT for DIF detection\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**:\n   - Is combining MFRM + DG-IRT gating novel enough for a top venue?\n   - Are the three adaptations (binary→continuous, single→multi-facet, known→unknown) individually non-trivial?\n   - Is Proposition 1 novel, or is it a straightforward application of omitted variable bias?\n   - Does the paper offer conceptual insight beyond \"joint > sequential\"?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**:\n   - Only 10 references total — is this sufficient?\n   - Missing important related work? Consider: other IRT-based LLM evaluation papers, DIF literature, test contamination in educational testing, Bayesian psychometric models\n   - Is the positioning against baselines fair and accurate?\n\n3. **Framing and Motivation**:\n   - Is the confounding argument compelling?\n   - Is the paper well-motivated for the NLP community?\n   - Does the introduction overclaim or appropriately scope?\n\n4. **Writing Quality**:\n   - Is the paper clearly written?\n   - Is the notation consistent?\n   - Is the paper well-organized?\n   - Any clarity issues?\n\n5. **Significance and Impact**:\n   - Will this change how the community does LLM evaluation?\n   - Is the practical barrier to adoption (8 judges, MCMC 3h) too high?\n   - Is the improvement (τ: 0.86→0.91) meaningful in practice?\n\n6. **Page Limit Compliance**:\n   - Main text is Sections 1-7 (Conclusion). Limitations is exempt.\n   - Does main text fit within 8 pages?\n\n7. **Anonymity and Format**:\n   - Any anonymity violations?\n   - Proper use of anonymous submission conventions?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n### Strengths (bullet list, 4-6 items)\n### Weaknesses (bullet list, 4-6 items, with severity: Major/Minor)\n### Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n### Novelty Assessment (detailed paragraph)\n### Related Work Gaps\n### Soundness Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Excitement Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Reproducibility Score (1-5, with justification)\n### Preliminary Overall Assessment (1-5 scale, 0.5 increments, calibrated to EMNLP acceptance rate)\n### Confidence (1-5)\n","agentId":"a7f5ddd906765a49a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have all the information needed to write a thorough review. Here is my assessment:\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 -- Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n**Paper**: \"G-MFRM: Joint Estimation of Judge Bias and Benchmark Contamination for Fair LLM Evaluation\"\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated problem formulation.** The paper identifies a genuine gap: no prior work (0/12 surveyed papers) jointly models judge severity bias and benchmark contamination. The omitted variable confounding framing is clean and immediately communicates why sequential correction is insufficient. The toy example in Section 2.3 is effective pedagogy.\n\n- **Principled psychometric grounding.** Combining MFRM with DG-IRT gating is a reasonable and well-motivated design choice. The three-stage estimation pipeline (MFRM init, exposure anchoring, joint Bayesian estimation) addresses the identifiability challenge of unknown exposure status in a structured way. The paper correctly identifies that educational testing DG-IRT assumes known exposure, and the external anchoring via ConStat is a non-trivial adaptation.\n\n- **Honest reporting of effect sizes.** The paper does not hide that the empirical improvement is modest (delta-tau = 0.02 on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard; non-significant on WildBench). The conclusion explicitly acknowledges this and reframes the contribution around methodological correctness and diagnostic value. This is a strength of scientific integrity.\n\n- **Graceful degradation property.** Demonstrating that G-MFRM reduces to standard MFRM when contamination is absent eliminates an important adoption concern. The WildBench results (12% high-exposure items, non-significant improvement) serve as a natural \"negative control.\"\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental design.** 8 judges from 4 model families, 22 target models, 203K evaluations, position swapping, ablation study, judge count sensitivity, adversarial robustness, cross-benchmark validation -- this is thorough for a methods paper.\n\n- **Interpretable outputs.** Per-model c_n estimates with cross-benchmark consistency (r = 0.68--0.71), per-judge severity profiles, and per-item difficulty -- these have standalone diagnostic value beyond ranking correction.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Proposition 1 is a restatement of classical omitted variable bias, not a novel theoretical contribution.** The bias expression Cov(d_j, c_n * e_i) / Var(d_j) is the textbook OVB formula from econometrics (Angrist & Pischke 2009, Chapter 3; or any introductory econometrics text). The claim that this \"follows from standard omitted variable bias analysis adapted to the psychometric setting\" (Section 2 of the abstract summary) is accurate but undermines the novelty claim. Calling this a \"Proposition\" with Monte Carlo \"validation\" overstates the contribution -- the OVB formula does not need 5000 simulations to validate; it is an algebraic identity under the assumed linear model. The paper should clearly attribute this to the OVB literature and position the contribution as *applying* this known result to identify a previously unrecognized confounding in LLM evaluation, rather than presenting it as a new theoretical result.\n\n- **(Major) Extremely thin related work with only ~10 references.** For a paper that bridges psychometrics, LLM evaluation, and contamination detection, 10 references is far below the expected density for EMNLP. Critical missing citations include: (1) Polo et al. 2024 \"TinyBenchmarks\" (IRT for efficient LLM evaluation, ICML 2024) -- directly relevant as an IRT-based LLM evaluation paper; (2) Sedoc & Ungar 2020 (item response theory for NLP) -- foundational IRT-in-NLP work; (3) Wang et al. 2025 \"Comparing Human and AI Rater Effects Using MFRM\" (arXiv 2505.18486) -- the most direct predecessor applying MFRM to LLM raters, which the literature_map.yaml lists as the \"most direct predecessor\" yet it is absent from the paper; (4) the DIF (differential item functioning) literature which is the psychometric analog of what G-MFRM does; (5) the broader test contamination literature from educational measurement (e.g., Eckerly 2017, test security and item exposure); (6) Fiedler 2026 on bias and uncertainty in LLM-as-a-judge. The paper's own literature map identifies 12 papers, but only ~10 appear in the references. This is a serious gap.\n\n- **(Major) Modest empirical improvement raises questions about practical significance.** Delta-tau = 0.02 on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard, with p = 0.008 and p = 0.041 respectively. The WildBench result is non-significant (p = 0.062). While the paper argues that methodological correctness matters independent of effect size, reviewers will reasonably ask: if real-world contamination is in the \"mild\" regime (mean c_n = 0.62, closer to MVP-001), is the joint estimation apparatus justified for a 0.02 tau improvement? The 3.2-hour MCMC runtime and 4-judge minimum requirement create a substantial practical barrier relative to the gain. The paper would benefit from a clearer demonstration of settings where the improvement is practically meaningful.\n\n- **(Minor) Discrepancy between abstract summary and paper content.** The task description states tau = 0.91, AUC = 0.94, WildBench tau = 0.89; the actual median paper shows tau = 0.86, detection = 0.68 overall, WildBench tau = 0.84. This appears to reflect different versions of the paper (the \"best\" dream scenario). The review is based on the actual paper tex, but the authors should ensure consistency across all presentation materials.\n\n- **(Minor) The \"gating\" terminology is misleading.** In DG-IRT, the gating mechanism involves a binary indicator that activates/deactivates a latent component. In G-MFRM, there is no actual gate -- the contamination term c_n * e_i is simply an additive component that is always active (just small when e_i is near zero). Calling this \"gated\" suggests a mixture model or threshold mechanism that does not exist. The paper should clarify this or use more precise terminology.\n\n- **(Minor) Model formulation switches between ordinal and continuous without clear justification.** Equation 1 uses logistic MFRM (ordinal categories with thresholds tau_k), but the abstract summary mentions \"Gaussian residuals\" and \"binary to continuous\" adaptation. The paper should clearly specify whether the final G-MFRM uses an ordinal or continuous response model, and justify the choice given that MT-Bench uses 1-10 integer scores.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On Proposition 1**: Can you clarify what is novel beyond standard OVB? If the linear model E[X_nij] = mu + theta_n - b_i - d_j + c_n * e_i + epsilon is assumed, the OVB formula is immediate. Is the contribution (a) showing that OVB applies in this psychometric setting, or (b) showing that d_j and c_n * e_i are empirically non-orthogonal? If (b), the |rho| = 0.37 finding is the actual contribution, not the Proposition.\n\n2. **On exposure estimation circularity**: ConStat computes contamination effects by comparing original vs. rephrased item performance. G-MFRM then uses these ConStat deltas as exposure indicators to estimate contamination susceptibility c_n. How do you ensure that errors in ConStat's contamination signal do not propagate through the G-MFRM pipeline? What is the sensitivity of c_n estimates to noise in e_i?\n\n3. **On the 4-judge minimum**: Table 7 shows that with 2 judges, G-MFRM has a 65% convergence rate and zero improvement. Many LLM evaluation setups use 1-3 judges. Can you discuss the identifiability conditions more formally -- what specifically about 4 judges enables separation of d_j from c_n * e_i?\n\n4. **On Wang et al. 2025 (MFRM for LLM raters)**: Your literature map identifies this as the \"most direct predecessor.\" Why is it not cited in the paper? How does G-MFRM relate to and extend their work?\n\n5. **On the choice of ground truth**: Chatbot Arena Elo is used as the gold standard, but Arena rankings themselves may be affected by contamination (models that perform well on benchmarks attract more Arena traffic). How sensitive are the Kendall-tau results to this potential circularity?\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n\nThe novelty is real but narrower than presented. The paper's contribution is best understood as a *useful transfer* rather than a fundamental methodological advance. The key insight -- that judge bias and contamination confound each other -- is correct and valuable for the LLM evaluation community, but the formalization via OVB is standard. The G-MFRM model itself is a relatively direct combination of MFRM (well-established in psychometrics since 1989) and DG-IRT gating (Robitzsch 2020 / Shu et al. 2013). The three adaptations (binary-to-continuous, single-to-multi-facet, known-to-unknown exposure) are individually incremental: the first two are straightforward extensions, and the third (external anchoring) is essentially \"run ConStat first, then use its output.\" The most novel aspect is arguably the *problem identification* -- demonstrating that the two correction communities are producing confounded estimates -- rather than the solution itself. This is sufficient for a solid contribution but not at the level of conceptual novelty that would merit a top-tier venue award.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n\nSignificant gaps for a paper at this intersection:\n\n1. **Wang et al. 2025 (arXiv 2505.18486)** -- MFRM applied to 10 LLM raters vs. human experts. Most direct predecessor; absence is notable.\n2. **Polo et al. 2024 \"TinyBenchmarks\"** (ICML 2024) -- IRT for efficient LLM evaluation; relevant as an IRT-in-LLM-evaluation paper.\n3. **Sedoc & Ungar 2020** -- Item response theory for NLP evaluation; foundational reference.\n4. **DIF literature** (Holland & Wainer 1993; Zumbo 1999) -- Differential item functioning is the psychometric concept that G-MFRM's contamination term operationalizes. This connection should be made explicit.\n5. **Educational test security** literature (Eckerly 2017; Yi et al. 2008) -- Test contamination has been studied for decades in educational measurement; the parallel should be drawn.\n6. **Fiedler 2026** (arXiv 2605.06939) -- Bias and uncertainty diagnostics for LLM-as-a-judge; directly related.\n7. **Soumik 2026** (arXiv 2604.23178) -- Systematic evaluation of bias mitigation strategies; empirical complement.\n8. **Lin 2026 PSN-IRT** (AAAI 2026) -- Neural 4PL IRT for LLM benchmarking; IRT-based baseline.\n9. **Bayesian IRT models** (e.g., Natesan et al. 2016) -- The Bayesian estimation approach used in Stage 3 has precedent.\n10. Total reference count should be 25-35 for a paper of this scope at EMNLP.\n\n### Soundness Score: 3 / 5\n\nThe theoretical contribution (Proposition 1) is correct but not novel -- it is a direct application of OVB. The experimental design is thorough, but the main improvement (delta-tau = 0.02) is marginal and the WildBench result is non-significant. Convergence diagnostics are adequately reported (R-hat < 1.10, ESS > 100, 0.075% divergent transitions), though the 8% of parameters with R-hat > 1.05 and the 18% contamination misfit rate suggest the model does not fully capture the data-generating process. The three-stage pipeline introduces dependence on ConStat that is not fully analyzed for error propagation.\n\n### Excitement Score: 3 / 5\n\nThe problem identification (confounding between judge bias and contamination correction) is genuinely useful for the LLM evaluation community. The framework is principled. However, the empirical evidence suggests that current contamination levels make the joint correction practically unnecessary (WildBench non-significant; mean c_n = 0.62 in the \"mild\" regime). The paper reads more as a monitoring tool for future contamination than a solution to a current pressing problem. The 4-judge minimum and 3-hour runtime limit immediate adoption. This is a solid \"Findings\" paper that identifies an important methodological issue, but the impact is currently bounded by the empirical reality that contamination is not yet severe enough for joint correction to matter much.\n\n### Reproducibility Score: 3 / 5\n\nThe three-stage pipeline is described at a reasonable level, but several implementation details are missing: the specific NumPyro model code, prior distributions for all parameters (only c_n's half-normal prior is mentioned), the composite exposure weighting scheme, and the ConStat rephrasing procedure. The 203K evaluations represent substantial API costs that limit replication. No code or data release is mentioned. The 8-judge panel with position swapping is well-specified.\n\n### Preliminary Overall Assessment: 3.0\n\nCalibration: This is a methodologically sound paper that identifies a real problem (confounding in sequential LLM evaluation correction) and proposes a principled solution. The problem identification and formalization are the primary contributions. However, the novelty is incremental (OVB + MFRM + DG-IRT combination), the empirical gains are modest (delta-tau = 0.02, non-significant on WildBench), the related work is substantially incomplete (10 references for a cross-disciplinary paper), and the practical impact is limited by the current mild contamination regime. This is appropriate for Findings -- a useful contribution to the evaluation methodology literature that does not reach the threshold for the main conference. To reach 3.5-4.0, the paper would need: (a) substantially expanded related work with proper positioning against Wang 2025, DIF literature, and IRT-in-NLP work; (b) either stronger empirical results or a more compelling case for why the methodological contribution alone justifies acceptance; (c) clearer scoping of Proposition 1 as applied OVB rather than a novel result.\n\n### Confidence: 4 / 5\n\nI am familiar with both the LLM evaluation literature and the psychometric models (IRT, MFRM, DIF) referenced in this paper. My main uncertainty is around the practical significance threshold -- whether delta-tau = 0.02 is considered meaningful in the LLM evaluation community, where leaderboard differences of this magnitude may or may not affect downstream decisions."}],"totalDurationMs":151092,"totalTokens":48021,"totalToolUseCount":15,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":284,"cache_read_input_tokens":44121,"output_tokens":3615,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":284},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3615,"cache_read_input_tokens":44121,"cache_creation_input_tokens":284,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":284,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016A3oEsXVTukhePEUf8TpLC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to run the full roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews (done - R1 and R2 have submitted their reviews)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing - share each reviewer's review with the other\nStep 3: Convergence - identify areas of agreement and disagreement\nStep 4: Synthesize into a final report\n\nLet me note the key points from each reviewer:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments)**:\n\n\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 4/5\n- Reproducibility: 3/5\n- Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference)\n- Key concerns: Gaussian likelihood for bounded data, three-stage pipeline self-contradiction, N=22 statistical fragility, Proposition 1 lacks formal proof\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work)**:\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 3/5\n- Reproducibility: 3/5\n- Overall: 3.0 (Findings)\n- Key concerns: Proposition 1 is standard OVB, only 10 references\n\nBoth reviewers are aligned on soundness and reproducibility concerns, though R1 found the work more exciting. The main tension is around Proposition 1—R1 wants a formal proof while R2 sees it as lacking novelty since it's essentially standard omitted variable bias.\n\nWait, I'm noticing something odd in R2's review. The specific numbers they cite—delta-tau of 0.02, mean c_n of 0.62, WildBench p-value of 0.062, 65% convergence with 2 judges—don't match what I'm seeing in the paper. The actual results show G-MFRM tau at 0.91 versus UDA's 0.86 with a delta of 0.05, mean c_n of 1.18, and WildBench tau of 0.89 with a much stronger p-value of 2.1e-4. It looks like R2 might be conflating results from different versions or tiers of the paper, which is throwing off their assessment of the empirical contribution. I see R2 confused the paper versions—it read the median-tier experiment files instead of the actual PDF being reviewed. The paper I'm evaluating has tau=0.91, c̄=1.18, and WildBench tau=0.89 with p=2.1e-4, which are the correct numbers to use when assessing R2's review accuracy.\n\nNow I'm moving into the roundtable phase where I'll cross-share both reviewers' perspectives, identify their points of agreement and disagreement, and synthesize everything into a unified final report. I can see they both recognize the problem's practical importance and the breadth of experimental coverage, though I need to work through where their assessments diverge given R2's version confusion. R1 says \"lacks formal proof,\" R2 says \"is a restatement of classical OVB\"\n4. Related work is thin (10 references)\n5. Soundness: both give 3/5\n6. Reproducibility: both give 3/5\n7. The DG-IRT adaptation is real but incremental\n8. The paper is well-written and well-organized\n\n**Areas of Disagreement:**\n1. Excitement: R1 gives 4/5, R2 gives 3/5\n2. Overall: R1 gives 3.5, R2 gives 3.0\n3. R2's empirical concerns are based on incorrect numbers (median version, not the paper being reviewed)\n4. R1 focuses more on methodological issues (Gaussian likelihood, pipeline self-contradiction), R2 focuses more on novelty scope\n\n**Reconciliation:**\nR2 misread the empirical results—the actual delta-tau is 0.05 versus UDA with WildBench showing statistical significance at p=2.1e-4, which substantially strengthens the practical impact claim. Once corrected, R2's dismissal of the improvements as modest largely disappears, though both reviewers' concerns about OVB novelty, sparse references, and the Gaussian likelihood assumption remain legitimate. Looking at the full picture—tau of 0.91 versus 0.86 for the best baseline, comprehensive experiments with strong ablations and cross-benchmark validation—the paper demonstrates meaningful empirical gains alongside its principled psychometric approach to identifying confounding between bias and contamination.\n\nNow I'm ready to finalize the report and submit it.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13567,"cache_read_input_tokens":65431,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13567},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b85d8bd-7258-4dfd-917f-e95cb5e144aa","uuid":"e6be1e2a-ed91-44dd-a881-3e3cc538234a"}
